🔹 EXAMPLE 1: valueOf() and Integer Cache (−128 to 127)
Code (conceptually correct version)
Integer i1 = Integer.valueOf(10);
Integer i2 = Integer.valueOf(10);

System.out.println(i1 == i2);        // true
System.out.println(i1.equals(i2));   // true

Integer x1 = new Integer(200);
Integer x2 = new Integer(200);

System.out.println(x1 == x2);        // false
System.out.println(x1.equals(x2));   // true

✅ Explanation (VERY IMPORTANT)
🔹 Why i1 == i2 is true?

Integer.valueOf() uses cache

Cache range: −128 to 127

Value 10 is inside cache

Both i1 and i2 point to same memory

📌 == → reference comparison → same memory → true

🔹 Why x1 == x2 is false?

new Integer() always creates new objects

Two different memory locations

📌 == → different references → false

🔹 Why equals() is always true?

Wrapper classes override equals()

It compares content, not memory

🔹 EXAMPLE 2: Autoboxing uses valueOf()
Code
Integer i1 = 10;
Integer i2 = 10;

System.out.println(i1 == i2);        // true
System.out.println(i1.equals(i2));   // true

Integer x1 = 200;
Integer x2 = 200;

System.out.println(x1 == x2);        // false
System.out.println(x1.equals(x2));   // true

✅ Explanation

Autoboxing internally calls Integer.valueOf()

So same cache rules apply

Value	Inside Cache?	== result
10	yes	true
200	no	false

📌 equals() → always content comparison → true

🔹 EXAMPLE 3: String ➜ Primitive (parseXXX())
Code
String s1 = "100";
String s2 = "200";

System.out.println(s1 + s2); // 100200

int num = Integer.parseInt(s1);
float f = Float.parseFloat(s2);

System.out.println(num + f); // 300.0

✅ Explanation

"100" + "200" → string concatenation

parseXXX() converts String → primitive

After conversion → numeric addition

📌 Rule:

parseXXX() → String → primitive

🔹 EXAMPLE 4: Number Parent Class
Code
void disp(Number n) {
    System.out.println(n);
}

disp(Integer.valueOf(10));
disp(Float.valueOf(10.4f));

✅ Explanation

Number is parent class

Integer, Float, Double, etc. extend Number

Parent reference can hold child object

📌 This is polymorphism

🔹 EXAMPLE 5: compareTo() Method
Code
Integer i1 = Integer.valueOf(10);
Integer i2 = Integer.valueOf(20);
Integer i3 = Integer.valueOf(20);

System.out.println(i1.compareTo(i2)); // -1
System.out.println(i2.compareTo(i1)); // 1
System.out.println(i2.compareTo(i3)); // 0

✅ Explanation
Comparison	Result
smaller.compareTo(bigger)	-1
bigger.compareTo(smaller)	1
equal.compareTo(equal)	0

📌 Used for sorting

🔹 EXAMPLE 6: String ➜ Primitive (Multiple Types)
Code
String s1 = "100";
String s2 = "200";

int a = Integer.parseInt(s1);
float b = Float.parseFloat(s2);
double c = Double.parseDouble(s2);

System.out.println(a + c); // 300.0

✅ Explanation

parseXXX() exists in all wrapper classes

Converts String → primitive

Output is numeric addition

🔹 EXAMPLE 7: Autoboxing + Number Parent
Code
void disp(Number n) {
    System.out.println(n);
}

disp(10);      // int → Integer → Number
disp(10.4f);   // float → Float → Number

✅ Explanation

Primitive → wrapper (autoboxing)

Wrapper → parent (Number)

Happens automatically

📌 This is autoboxing + polymorphism

🧠 FINAL SUPER-EASY MEMORY MAP
valueOf()   → primitive/string → wrapper
parseXXX()  → string → primitive
xxxValue()  → wrapper → primitive
==           → memory comparison
equals()    → content comparison
compareTo() → sorting (-1, 0, 1)

🎯 INTERVIEW ONE-LINER (CONFIDENT ANSWER)

“Integer caching works from −128 to 127 and is used by valueOf and autoboxing.
== compares references, equals() compares content.
parseXXX() converts String to primitive, and compareTo() is used for sorting.”